data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is required.**

Your code uses only `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — none of which are affected by the .NET 6+ breaking change. These APIs work identically on .NET 8. The code will compile and run correctly as-is.

---

**Optional modernization (unrelated to Thread.Abort):** You could replace the manual thread management with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.WhenAll` for cleaner code and better thread pool utilization:

```csharp
public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken token = default)
{
    await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, token, async (item, ct) =>
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        await Task.Delay(100, ct); // replaces Thread.Sleep
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

But again — this is purely optional. Your existing code needs no changes to run on .NET 8.
